※ pl4g-test: compile=error
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4205 LANG_TYPE_OPERAND_NOT_INTEGER

※ Every piece of an expansion keeps the position it was written at.  That falls out
※ of substituting trees rather than text: a tree from the caller carries the
※ caller's spans and one from the template carries the macro's, because those are
※ the spans the parser gave them.
※
※ So an error in an expanded program names the place the offending text was
※ actually written, which is either the invocation or the macro -- and those are
※ the only two answers that can be right.  Here it is the *caller*: the macro is
※ fine and `true` is what was handed to it.

macro twice:
    ⌜$x⌝ → ⌜$x + $x⌝

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(twice⌜true⌝, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
